'''''Zantedeschia albomaculata''''', commonly called the '''spotted calla lily'''<ref>{{PLANTS|id=ZAAL|taxon=Zantedeschia albomaculata|access-date=9 August 2015}}</ref> (although ''[[Calla]]'' is a genus unto itself) or the '''white spotted arum''', is a species of [[flowering plant]] in the arum family, [[Araceae]].<ref>{{cite web |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=n.d. |title=Zantedeschia albomaculata (Hook.) Baill. |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:89405-1 |access-date=July 10, 2020 |website=Plants of the World Online |publisher=The Trustees of the Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew}}
</ref> Its leaves are somewhat smaller, thinner and slightly more pointed than the larger ''[[Zantedeschia aethiopica]]''—also commonly called the "calla lily"—and display attractive white speckling on their faces. The plant spreads laterally underground by way of a [[rhizome]] or [[tuber]], with nodes (or "eyes") that sprout and send-up new shoots. Its [[inflorescence]] is typical of the Araceae family, featuring a white, funnel-shaped [[Bract|spathe]] (or bract) with a yellowish [[Spadix (botany)|spadix]] in the center.  

In modern cultivation, numerous color variations have been developed, ranging from white with splashes of other colors to bright yellow through to deep purple, almost black, spathes. Various forms are commonly sold in stores and garden centers, both as potted plants or as bare-root rhizomes to be planted outdoors. 

